= Using the arc4 class =

The [../classes/html/classarc4.html arc4 class] provides the RC4 stream cipher.

Unlike the classes derived from the [../classes/html/classencryption.html encryption class], arc4 is not a block cipher.  It has no key size restrictions, no initialization vector, no padding, and no block cipher mode.  Instead, each instance is constructed with a key (or given one via setKey()), and generates a running keystream from it.  The crypt() method XORs that keystream against a buffer, in place, advancing the keystream as it goes.  Since RC4 encryption and decryption are the same operation, there is only one method, crypt(), used for both.

Each instance keeps its own independent, running keystream, so two instances constructed from the same key can be used concurrently without interfering with each other - for example, one to encrypt outgoing data and another to decrypt incoming data on a duplex stream.  crypt() may be called repeatedly, buffer by buffer, across the life of the stream, and the keystream simply continues from where the previous call left off.

The isSupported() method always returns true, since a fallback implementation is built in for platforms where !OpenSSL isn't available.

The following example constructs two instances from the same key, one per direction, and uses them to encrypt outgoing data and decrypt incoming data.

